  <bp:ProteinReference xmlns:bp="http://www.biopax.org/release/biopax-level3.owl#" xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" rdf:ID="ProteinReference71">
    <bp:organism rdf:resource="#BioSource1" />
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">UniProt:Q96BZ9 TBC1D20</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">TBC1D20</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">C20orf140</bp:name>
    <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref795" />
    <bp:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION GTPase-activating protein (GAP) specific for Rab1 and Rab2 small GTPase families for which it can accelerate the intrinsic GTP hydrolysis rate by more than five orders of magnitude (PubMed:23236136). Also shows GAP activity for RAB18 GTPase (PubMed:26063829). Promotes RAB18 dissociation from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane into the cytosol, probably through stimulating RAB18 GTP-hydrolysis (PubMed:26063829). Involved in maintaining endoplasmic reticulum structure (PubMed:24891604).SUBUNIT (Microbial infection) Directly interacts with the N-terminal amphipathic helix of hepatitis C virus (HCV) NS5A.INTERACTION The arginine and glutamine fingers are critical for the GTPase-activating mechanism, they pull out Rab's 'switch 2' glutamine and insert in Rab's active site.DISEASE The disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry.MISCELLANEOUS May be due to intron retention.</bp:comment>
  </bp:ProteinReference>
